  "final_message": "I put `channel` back in both the order model and the `OrderPlaced` event. The CI failure showed that `analytics-ingest` still reads that field and requires it to be one of `web | store | marketplace`. So the idea that nothing reads `channel` was wrong, and the only way to pass this check from our side is to keep publishing it. The event gets the value from the order, so the model needs it too.\n\n**What I did**\n- My earlier change had been committed as `1401714 gen`. I tried to restore the files from `base` with `git checkout`, but it was blocked for needing approval, so I reverted the changes by hand.\n- Removing `channel` from the request had also turned it into an optional, ignored field. That's undone too: `POST /orders` again requires `channel` and returns 400 for an unsupported value. I deleted the test I'd added for ignoring the old field, since it no longer applies.\n- `git diff 4b85279` shows no content differences, so the working tree is identical to `base`. Git only warns about line endings.\n\n**Verification:** Type-check, the dependency-cruiser checks and the 39 unit tests all pass. I couldn't run the ARCH-4 contract check locally because its consumer contracts aren't in this repo. It should pass now that the event is back to its original shape, but CI will confirm.\n\nNothing is committed. To finish, you could commit this as a revert of `gen`, or drop `gen` from the branch.\n\nIf you still want `channel` gone, the analytics-ingest team would first have to stop requiring it in their contract. Then the removal could go out, probably as `OrderPlaced` v4.",
